// Costume Drama: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== costume-drama.f01 | feature | three-act
title: The Seamstress Of The Congress
genre: costume-drama + romance
tone: Candlelit, rustling and sharply observed
era: 1814 | period
setting: Vienna during the great peace congress | real | city
logline: While emperors redraw Europe at balls that never end, a dressmaker's forewoman who fits the wives and mistresses of every delegation discovers that she knows, from who orders what for which evening, more about the negotiations than the secret police who are paying her to tell them.
premise: A hundred thousand visitors have doubled the city. Nothing is decided in session; everything is decided at supper, in a waltz, in a carriage. The police minister has three hundred informers going through wastepaper. {resi} runs the workroom of the most fashionable modiste in the city. A gown takes six fittings. At a fitting a woman stands still for an hour, in her shift, and talks.
synopsis: The first act establishes that clothes are intelligence. A duchess who orders mourning the week before a death is announced; a mistress who changes her colours from one emperor's to another's; a delegation that suddenly cannot pay. {resi} reads these as a matter of trade, to manage credit. A police agent, {siber}, courteous and exhausted, notices her accounts and makes an arrangement: a small pension for a weekly conversation. She agrees, to keep the workroom safe. The second act is the Congress at full swirl: sleigh parties, a tournament in medieval dress, a ball for ten thousand, and behind it the question of which lands go to which crown. {resi} is fitting two rival hostesses, the witty {the_duchess}, who serves one great power, and a princess who serves another, and understands before the ministers do that a secret alliance has been made, because three women ordered travelling furs on the same morning. She also has a private matter. A young secretary in a minor delegation, {jan}, a Pole whose country is the thing being divided, comes about a waistcoat and stays to argue; he is the only person in the city who asks what she thinks. What she tells the police could ruin his cause. What she tells him could hang her. She begins, with great care, to edit: the police get what is true and useless. In the third act the dancing stops overnight: the exile has left his island. Delegations pack. Unpaid bills are an avalanche. {siber} comes for a last report and lets her know that he has always known what she left out, and wrote it down as nothing. {jan}'s country is given away at a stroke in an afternoon session. He leaves with the waistcoat unpaid for and a letter she will not answer, because the workroom has forty women in it. The last scene is spring: a new client, the fittings begin again, and {resi} at the mirror pins a hem and listens.
question: What do you owe the powerful when you can see them in their shifts?
themes: women's knowledge as the unofficial record; the peace of the great made from the lives of the small; discretion as power
audience: Adults who like wit, silk and politics
viewpoint: With {resi}, at the level of hems and mirrors; the sovereigns are glimpsed
stakes: A workroom of forty women, a man's cause, and her neck
conflict: A dressmaker's loyalty to her trade and her people against a police state and a heart she cannot afford
ending: The Congress breaks up. The country is divided. She keeps the workroom, loses the man, and was protected by the spy.
experience: Gorgeous surfaces, quick talk, and a quiet bruise
look: Silk under candlelight, mirrors, pattern paper; snow on sleighs; a ballroom seen from the cloakroom
sound: Scissors, pins in a saucer, a waltz through a wall. Carriage wheels all night.
m: protect | institution | love-vs-duty | bittersweet
@ resi | Therese Hollaus | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 32 | The workroom safe and paid | Forty women eat by it | She knows too much and is being paid to say it | Shrewd, tactful, dryly funny, unsentimental about her betters | From tradeswoman to editor of the secret record | Viennese, polite, exact about money | Black silk, a pincushion at the wrist, tape round her neck
@ jan | Jan Radziwonski | co-lead | love-interest | human | he/him | 27 | His country on the map | He promised his father | It is the bargaining chip of four powers | Earnest, argumentative, ill-dressed | From hope to exile, with a waistcoat unpaid | Fast, accented French; asks questions | One good coat, ink on his cuffs
@ siber | Baron Siber | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 55 | Reports by Friday | The emperor reads them at breakfast | Three hundred informers and nothing but wastepaper | Weary, civil, more humane than his office | From paymaster to quiet protector | Soft, bureaucratic | Grey coat, a portfolio, a cough
@ the_duchess | The Duchess | supporting | foil | human | she/her | 21 | Her uncle's success and her own amusement | She is the cleverest hostess in the city | She talks at fittings | Brilliant, careless, kind when it costs nothing | From client to a carriage leaving at dawn | Rapid, sparkling | Muslin, cashmere shawls, never the same gown twice
~ resi - jan | dressmaker and client | a waistcoat | a letter left unanswered
~ resi - siber | informant and police minister | a pension for conversation | he knew what she left out
~ resi - the_duchess | forewoman and client | pins and gossip | a bill settled as a farewell kindness
# Fittings | INT | Modiste's Fitting Room | DAY | resi, the_duchess | Listen | A woman in her shift on a stool, six mirrors, an hour of talk. {resi} has pins in her mouth and cannot reply. She hears who cannot pay and who has changed colours | | 6 | props=gown+pins+mirrors
# Fittings | INT | Workroom Office | NIGHT | resi, siber | Be recruited | He admires her ledgers: she knew of a death before the court did, from an order for crape. A small pension. A weekly talk. She thinks of forty women | {resi} agrees to inform | 5 | props=ledgers
# The Dance | INT | Imperial Ballroom, Cloakroom Gallery | NIGHT | resi, jan | Watch from above | Ten thousand candles. She is there with needle and thread for torn hems. He is there because nobody will see him downstairs. He asks what she thinks will happen to Poland | They meet properly | 7 | crowd=800, dance, props=sewing kit
# The Dance | INT | Workroom | DAY | resi | Read the furs | Three orders for travelling furs, the same morning, from three delegations that do not speak. She lays the slips side by side. An alliance | She understands the secret treaty | 5 | crowd=40, props=order slips+furs
# The Dance | INT | Workroom Office | NIGHT | resi, siber | Edit | The weekly talk. She gives him a countess's debts and a tenor's affair, all true. She does not mention furs. He writes steadily | She protects him | 5 | props=portfolio
# The Dance | EXT | Sleigh Party, Palace Park | DAY | resi, jan, the_duchess | Be seen together | Thirty gilded sleighs. She rides at the back with a hatbox. He walks beside. The duchess, passing, notices everything and says only that the waistcoat suits him | | 5 | crowd=150, animals=horses, vehicles=sleighs, vfx=snow
# The End Of The Music | INT | Ballroom | NIGHT | resi, the_duchess, jan | Stop | A messenger. A whisper travelling round the floor. The orchestra plays four more bars to an emptying room. He has left his island | The Congress breaks | 6 | crowd=600, dance
# The End Of The Music | INT | Workroom Office | DAY | resi, siber | Hear what he knew | Trunks in the street. He settles her pension to the day. He mentions that travelling furs were much worn this winter, and that he found it not worth recording | | 5 | props=portfolio
# The End Of The Music | INT | Modiste's Fitting Room | DAY | resi | Begin again | Spring. A letter in her pocket, unanswered. A new client on the stool. She kneels to the hem with pins in her mouth and listens | | 3 | props=gown+pins+mirrors
review: authored | 2026-11-12 | written whole; drawn from the peace congress of 1814 to 1815 and its police reports, with invented people
